shortAnswer: "A context window is the total amount of text an AI model can consider at one time — your instructions, the conversation so far, any documents you've attached, and its own previous answers. When the window fills up, the oldest material falls out of view. That's why a long conversation can start contradicting itself or 'forgetting' something you said earlier."
faq:
  - q: "How big is a context window?"
    a: "It varies by model and changes often — the leading models handle the equivalent of several hundred pages in one go. The number matters less than the behaviour: every model has a limit, and quality degrades as you approach it."
  - q: "Why did the AI forget what I told it ten minutes ago?"
    a: "Either the conversation outgrew the window and the earliest part dropped out, or the detail was buried among so much other material that it carried little weight. Both are fixed the same way — restate the important constraint rather than assuming it's still held."
  - q: "Does a bigger context window always mean better answers?"
    a: "No. Filling a large window with loosely relevant material often produces <em>worse</em> answers than a small, well-chosen set of information. Relevance beats volume. A large window is useful headroom, not a target to fill."
  - q: "Does the AI remember between conversations?"
    a: "By default, no — a new conversation starts empty. Some products add a memory feature that carries selected facts across sessions, but that's a feature built on top, not the context window itself."
  - q: "What's the practical fix for long projects?"
    a: "Work in focused sessions rather than one endless thread, and start each with a short brief of the decisions already made. You're re-supplying the context that matters instead of hoping it survived."
